:::


## Our results

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/dog_spatial_results_blank.png){height="550" fig-align="center"}

::: {data-id="box1" style="background: #132E53; width: 500px; height: 600px; margin: 10px; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 515px"}
:::

![](media/dog_spatial_results.png){.fragment height="550" fig-align="center"}

::: {data-id="box1" style="background: #132E53; width: 500px; height: 600px; margin: 10px; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 515px"}
:::

![](media/dog_spatial_results.png){.fragment height="550" fig-align="center"}

:::

::: {.source}
[Stevens et al. 2022, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.09.03.02.2022)
:::


## Study conclusions

::: {.incremental}
* Owners not good at predicting dog impulsivity

* Did not replicate Brady et al., 2018

  - British know their dogs better than Americans
  <!-- - Poor attempt at replication -->
  - Different dog populations (breeds, neuter status)
  - Different owner populations
  - Less than robust methods in original study

:::


## Implications

::: {.incremental}
* Increase sample sizes
* Conduct more replications (within and between labs)
* Increase robustness and reproducibility of analyses
* Post data publicly
* Provide more and consistent info on owners
* Consider populations of owners and dogs

:::



# Big team science


## What is big team science?

> Collaborations wherein a comparatively large number of researchers pool their intellectual and/or material resources to pursue a common goal

---Baumgartner et al., 2023 _Royal Society Open Science_.


## Why big team science?

* Larger sample sizes
* Less effort per research site
* Consensus in methods design
* Diverse subject pool
* Many analysts involved in analysis
<!-- * Shared labor in data validation and code checking -->


## ManyDogs Project

International research consortium of scientists with shared interest in canine behavior and cognition

![](media/manydogs_banner.jpg){fig-align="center" height="300"}


## ManyDogs Project Aims

::: {.incremental}
1. Attempt to replicate important studies
2. Investigate moderators that require large sample sizes
3. Develop consensus on methodological best practices
4. Investigate cultural differences
5. Set the bar for replicability of studies
:::


## ManyDogs 1

::: {.callout-tip}
## Research Question

::: {style="font-size: 40px"}
::: {.fragment}
Do dogs treat human pointing as communicative cues?
:::
:::
:::

::: {.fragment}
![](media/dog-pointing.jpg){fig-align="center" width="550"}
:::


## Results

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/md1_overall_accuracy_0.png){fig-align="center" height="550"}

![](media/md1_overall_accuracy_2.png){fig-align="center" height="550" .fragment}

![](media/md1_overall_accuracy_4_last.png){fig-align="center" height="550" .fragment}
:::

::: {.source}
[ManyDogs Project et al. 2023, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.10.03.03.2023)
:::


## Site effects

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/md1_site_accuracy_1.png){fig-align="center" height="550"}

![](media/md1_site_accuracy_2_last.png){fig-align="center" height="550" .fragment}
:::

::: {.source}
[ManyDogs Project et al. 2023, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.10.03.03.2023)
:::


## Site differences

![](media/md1_owner_ages.png){fig-align="center" height="550"}

::: {.source}
[ManyDogs Project et al. 2023, _Animal Behavior & Cognition_](https://doi.org/10.26451/abc.10.03.03.2023)
:::


## Study conclusions

::: {.incremental}
* Dogs were slightly better than chance but no difference between conditions

* Consistent findings across sites

* Results do not match robust literature showing point following

* Likely due to method choices

<!-- * Big differences in owner age distribution -->

:::


## Implications
::: {.incremental}
* Again, consider populations of owners and dogs
* Many analysts improved analysis
* Diverse viewpoints can bias methods
* Consider multi-site replication

:::



# Replicating interpretations


## Dog quantity preferences

::: {.callout-tip}
## Research Question

::: {style="font-size: 40px"}
::: {.fragment}
What numerical factors contribute to dog quantity preferences?
:::
:::
:::

::: {.fragment}
![](media/preference_stim.jpg){fig-align="center" height="350"}
:::


## What drives preference? 

### Numerical ratio (small / large) or difference (large - small)

:::{.r-stack}

:::{.fragment}

```{r}
library(gt)
ratio_pairs <- tibble(Small = c(1, 2, 1, 2), Large = c(2, 4, 3, 6), Ratio = Small / Large, Difference = Large - Small)
ratio_pairs |> 
  gt() |> 
  tab_options(column_labels.padding.horizontal = 50, table.font.size = "120%", table.font.names = "Arial", column_labels.font.weight = "bold") |>   
  cols_align("center") |> 
  fmt_number(columns = Ratio, decimals = 2)
```
:::

:::{.fragment}
```{r}
ratio_pairs |> 
  gt() |> 
  tab_options(column_labels.padding.horizontal = 50, table.font.size = "120%", table.font.names = "Arial", column_labels.font.weight = "bold") |>   
  cols_align("center") |> 
  fmt_number(columns = Ratio, decimals = 2) |> 
  data_color(rows = Ratio == 0.5,
             palette = "#425875") |> 
  data_color(rows = Ratio == 1/3,
             palette = "#718298")
```
:::

:::{.fragment}
```{r}
ratio_pairs |> 
  gt() |> 
  tab_options(column_labels.padding.horizontal = 50, table.font.size = "120%", table.font.names = "Arial", column_labels.font.weight = "bold") |>   
  cols_align("center") |> 
  fmt_number(columns = Ratio, decimals = 2) |> 
  data_color(rows = Difference == 2,
             palette = "#425875") |> 
  data_color(rows = Difference == 1,
             palette = "#718298") |> 
  data_color(rows = Difference == 4,
             palette = "steelblue4")

```
:::
:::


## Set-up

![](media/number_setup.png)

::: {.source}
[deBoer et al. PsyArXiv](https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/rn8gq)
:::


## Results

::: {.r-stack}
![](media/hypothesis1.1.png){fig-align="center" height="550"}

::: {data-id="box1" style="background: #132E53; width: 500px; height: 600px; margin: 10px; position: absolute; top: 50px; left: 590px"}
:::

![](media/hypothesis1.1.png){.fragment fig-align="center" height="550"}

![](media/hypothesis1.2.png){fig-align="center" height="550" .fragment}
:::

::: {.source}
[deBoer et al. PsyArXiv](https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/rn8gq)
:::


## Previous work on difference and ratio

| Study                                          | Ratio effects | Difference effects |
|-------------------------------------|:-------------------:|:------------------------:|
| Ward &amp; Smuts 2007                          |         Yes         |            Yes           |
| Baker et al. 2012                              |         Yes         |        Not tested        |
| Miletto Petrazzini &amp; Wynn 2016 |         Yes         |        Not tested        |
| Aulet et al. 2019 |         Yes         |        Not tested        |
| Rivas-Blanco et al. 2020                       |         Yes         |        Not tested        |


## Study conclusions

::: {.incremental}
* Both difference and ratio account for numerical preference when analyzed separately

* But only difference accounts when analyzed together

* Revisit ubiquity of ratio effects

* Include difference in models and design studies to test difference effects 

:::


## Implications
::: {.incremental}
* Question dogma
* Be open to alternative factors at play
* Use strong inference and triangulation
* Analyze other people's data

:::



